WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.489
Lifetime defects can be intermittent because stale access may appear to work until timing, allocation layout, compiler settings, or container

2
00:00:07.489 --> 00:00:08.238
behavior changes.

3
00:00:08.238 --> 00:00:10.859
That makes ordinary happy-path testing insufficient.

4
00:00:10.859 --> 00:00:13.480
Tests need to exercise lifecycle boundaries directly.

5
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.224
Sanitizers can reveal memory lifetime defects during development and CI.

6
00:00:17.224 --> 00:00:26.211
They can make invalid access visible close to the operation that triggers it, which is far easier to debug than a later corrupted state.

7
00:00:26.211 --> 00:00:31.453
These tools do not replace design, but they provide valuable feedback while tests run.

8
00:00:31.453 --> 00:00:37.070
Debug iterator modes, hardened library settings, compiler diagnostics, and static analysis can add useful coverage.

9
00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:43.810
They may catch invalid container access, suspicious lifetime patterns, or dangerous captures before the issue appears in production.

10
00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:48.678
Tool settings should be documented so developers know which builds provide which checks.

11
00:00:48.678 --> 00:00:54.669
Tests should cover destruction order, container mutation, move operations, callback cancellation, asynchronous completion, observer removal, and

12
00:00:54.669 --> 00:00:55.418
error paths.

13
00:00:55.418 --> 00:01:00.286
These are the situations where access often outlives the object it depends on.

14
00:01:00.286 --> 00:01:04.404
Boundary lifecycle tests are just as important as boundary value tests.

15
00:01:04.404 --> 00:01:11.519
Tool findings should become tracked engineering defects with an owner, a fix, a regression test, and follow-up review.

16
00:01:11.519 --> 00:01:13.766
Suppressions should be rare and documented.

17
00:01:13.766 --> 00:01:20.880
A team gets stronger lifetime safety when diagnostics become part of normal delivery rather than a separate cleanup campaign.
